<!--Post: 1114--><!--Time: 1097164418-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1114"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, October 07, 2004 - 8:53 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There have been various speculations about BTK&#39;s victims. Why her? What did she do to set him off, etc.? From the books that I have read, serial killers think that the victims picked them. The killers perceive a trait or set of traits in their victim&#40;s&#41;, and the serial killer thinks that the person is aware of those traits and is essentially attracting or luring the killer. Very convoluted thinking. Of course, there are victims of opportunity as well. BTK&#39;s known victims have been wholesome and/or family type persons. <BR> <BR>The victims may tell more about BTK than his MO and signature. BTK is a savage, vicious, brutal killer in his &#39;serial killing&#39; profession. The same was true of Dean Coryll; yet, those who knew him said that he would give someone the shirt off his back to help them. Only BTK can give an explanation, and I would wager that he does not have insight into why he kills.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1114-->
<!--Post: 1126--><!--Time: 1097288417-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1126"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, October 08, 2004 - 7:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>&#34;Only BTK can give an explanation, and I would wager that he does not have insight into why he kills.&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t think any of them do. They may speak of monsters, slaves in paradise, &#34;factor x,&#34; a neighbor&#39;s dog or ask for &#34;help,&#34; and other BS; I think BTK just wants to stay in the headlines at this point. As time passes, traditional profiling <BR>beliefs, some of them, are becoming less impressive or have almost been disproved. The shirt-off-his-back thing applies also to Bundy, Gacy and others. I don&#39;t think it means much one way or the other. <BR> <BR>Victimology: While his victims include two children and both sexes, I still feel as though BTK preferred adult women. I don&#39;t know if there&#39;s much beyond that. I really don&#39;t. There are the letters, the single most important thing about which may simply the fact that they are being sent in the first place. He&#39;s no Zodiac. I think BTK&#39;s letters are derivative, boring, and to me they feel <i>forced</i>. Anyway, except for Coleman, and even that may be shaky, there isn&#39;t a lot which connects the victims. Except that they didn&#39;t live far apart from each other. And that their slayer should have been captured a long time ago. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1126-->
